arXiv:2605.28367v2 [cs.RO] 28 May 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
Faisal Lawan, Xiaoran Han, Joaquin Carrasco, Barry Lennox, Xiaoxiao Cheng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes">This work was supported by the RAICo Programme and the University of Manchester Strategic Investment Reserve Fund.F. Lawan, X. Han, J. Carrasco, B. Lennox, and X. Cheng are with the Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The University of Manchester, M13 9PL, United Kingdom, (Corresponding authors: F. Lawan, <span id="id1.1.id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">faisal.lawan@manchester.ac.uk</span>; X. Cheng, <span id="id2.2.id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xiaoxiao.cheng@manchester.ac.uk</span>.)</span></span>
</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
<p class="ltx_p">Safe physical interaction is critical for deploying robotic manipulators in human–robot interaction and contact-rich tasks, where uncertainty, external forces, and actuator limitations can compromise both performance and safety. We propose an online adaptive impedance control framework that enforces joint-state safety while achieving compliant interaction under uncertain dynamics. The approach combines a quadratic-program-based safety filter with a novel composed position–velocity non-smooth control barrier function, enabling joint position and velocity constraints to be enforced through a unified relative-degree-one barrier. Unknown dynamics are compensated online using an interval type-2 fuzzy logic system, while actuator torque limits are handled through soft constraints with exact penalty recovery of feasible solutions. A disturbance-observer-enhanced safety mechanism improves robustness against modelling errors and external interaction forces. Using composite Lyapunov analysis, we prove forward invariance of the safe set and the uniform ultimately boundedness of the impedance-tracking error. Simulations on a 7-DOF manipulator with severe parametric uncertainty and external interaction wrenches demonstrate safe constraint satisfaction and robust impedance tracking.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span>
</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Physical human–robot interaction (HRI) requires robots to operate safely in proximity to humans while interacting with uncertain and dynamic environments. Impedance control is therefore critical, as it enables robots to regulate the dynamic relationship between motion and interaction forces, allowing safe and compliant contact with humans and uncertain environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="Impedance Control: An Approach to Manipulation" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. It becomes particularly important in tasks such as surface interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="Force, Impedance, and Trajectory Learning for Contact Tooling and Haptic Identification" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, haptic sensing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="Object Recognition Using Mechanical Impact, Viscoelasticity, and Surface Friction During Interaction" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and operator assistance in remote handling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="Robot-Mediated Asymmetric Connection Between Humans Can Improve Performance Without Increasing Effort" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, where accurate dynamic models are unavailable and stable contact must be maintained under uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, impedance control in dynamic HRI faces several key challenges in extreme environments such as nuclear decommissioning. First, robot manipulator dynamics are subject to significant uncertainties, necessitating adaptive regulation during interaction to accurately track the desired impedance behaviour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="Adaptive impedance control of manipulators in human-robot interaction" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Second, robotic joints are subject to safety constraints, including limits on joint position, velocity, and actuator torque, which must be explicitly incorporated into the control design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="A unified quadratic-programming-based dynamical system approach to joint torque optimization of physically constrained redundant manipulators" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Third, discrepancies in task perception and motion planning between the human and the robot can lead to dynamically inconsistent interaction phases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="Human impedance modulation to improve visuo-haptic perception" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, resulting in uncertain and potentially destabilising contact forces that further complicate safety enforcement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="Physical Human–Robot Interaction" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Incorporating both safety requirements and dynamic uncertainties into impedance control design for robotic manipulators remains a significant challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among model-based approaches to impedance control, computed torque control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and Model Predictive Control (MPC) are widely used. While MPC can explicitly handle constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="Model Predictive Impedance Control" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, its effectiveness relies on accurate knowledge of system dynamics, which is often unavailable in HRI applications due to varying human-induced loads and model mismatch. Adaptive control methods address this limitation by estimating uncertain dynamics and updating control parameters online. Classical adaptive schemes typically rely on the linear-in-the-parameters (LIP) assumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, whereas learning-based approaches, such as neural networks (NNs) and fuzzy logic systems (FLSs), relax this requirement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="Fuzzy logic approach for controlling uncertain and nonlinear systems: a comprehensive review of applications and advances" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. However, neither class of methods guarantees the satisfaction of state or input constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Control Barrier Function (CBF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="Data-Driven Safety Filters: Hamilton-Jacobi Reachability, Control Barrier Functions, and Predictive Methods for Uncertain Systems" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> provides an effective approach for embedding safety constraints directly into control design by ensuring forward invariance of a safe set. Although standard CBF approaches also require accurate knowledge of the dynamics, several extensions address model uncertainty by using worst-case bounds, disturbance observers, and data-driven estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="Data-Driven Safety Filters: Hamilton-Jacobi Reachability, Control Barrier Functions, and Predictive Methods for Uncertain Systems" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="Adaptive Deep Neural Network-Based Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. In particular, the Adaptive Deep Neural Network (ADNN)-based CBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="Adaptive Deep Neural Network-Based Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> enforces safety online under bounded uncertainty, but it is computationally intensive and limited to tasks with sufficient excitation. The framework in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="State-Constrained Online Adaptive Control for Robotic Manipulators" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> combines NNs with CBFs to enable safe online adaptive tracking of manipulators. However, it is restricted to tracking tasks, can induce chattering, and does not explicitly account for actuator torque constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address the problem of safe impedance tracking in human–robot interaction under multiple constraints, we propose an online adaptive controller that tracks a desired impedance model during human interaction while enforcing state safety and respecting actuator torque limits whenever hard feasibility holds, without requiring prior offline training data. An Interval Type-2 Fuzzy Logic System (IT2-FLS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="Fuzzy Control and Modeling: Analytical Foundations and Applications" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="Modern Adaptive Fuzzy Control Systems" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> is employed as the function approximator, chosen for its interpretable rule structure and transparent compensation behaviour.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed method operates as follows. A unified soft-constrained quadratic program (QP) with an exact penalty function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="Soft constraints and exact penalty functions in model predictive control" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> simultaneously enforces joint state constraints, formulated via novel non-smooth CBFs (NCBFs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, as hard constraints while softening actuator torque constraints. A DOB-based robust modification of the NCBFs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> robustifies the safety filter under model uncertainty. The QP output is then integrated into a command-driven reference model, adopted from model reference adaptive control (MRAC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="Stable Adaptive Systems" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, whose filtered reference-model tracking error drives the IT2-FLS adaptation online. Because the reference model is driven by the constrained QP output rather than the unconstrained nominal command, the filtered error <math id="S1.p6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects reference-model tracking mismatch rather than constraint enforcement, thereby decoupling the adaptation loop from the safety filter. By softening only the torque constraints with a sufficiently large penalty weight, the QP solution coincides with the hard-constrained solution whenever feasible and gracefully relaxes torque limits otherwise, thereby eliminating chattering due to infeasibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">primary contributions</span> of this work are as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A novel position-velocity NCBF is composed that encodes joint position and velocity limits in a single relative-degree-one barrier function, yielding a Lipschitz-continuous safety filter without requiring higher-order CBF constructions. This NCBF is embedded as a hard constraint within a unified soft-constrained QP, together with a DOB-based robust modification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, thereby enforcing joint position and velocity safety invariantly while accommodating dynamic uncertainties.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An IT2-FLS-based online adaptive controller that learns unknown structured dynamics in real time. A command-driven reference model, in the spirit of MRAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="Stable Adaptive Systems" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, integrates the QP output so that the filtered reference-model error <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects reference-model tracking mismatch only, ensuring constraint-aware learning. Exact penalty functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="Soft constraints and exact penalty functions in model predictive control" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> recover hard torque feasibility when the interaction wrench is admissible and avoid chattering under infeasibility.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A composite Lyapunov stability analysis proving forward invariance of the NCBF safe set <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, torque satisfaction on the admissible wrench set <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and Uniformly Ultimately Bounded (UUB) impedance tracking with tunable bounds.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.28" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations:</span> Let <math id="S1.p8.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\coloneqq[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\coloneqq[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p8.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}\coloneqq(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}\coloneqq(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the space of <math id="S1.p8.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Boldface variables denote vectors or matrices. The identity matrix of size <math id="S1.p8.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.p8.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟎</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently-size matrix of zeros. Let <math id="S1.p8.8.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\|\cdot\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\cdot\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> be the Euclidean norm for vectors and the induced 2-norm for matrices. Let <math id="S1.p8.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d]\coloneqq\{1,\dots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d]\coloneqq\{1,\dots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S1.p8.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p8.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\ \mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\ \mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p8.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\coloneqq[\mathbf{x}^{\top},\mathbf{y}^{\top}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\coloneqq[\mathbf{x}^{\top},\mathbf{y}^{\top}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. For vectors <math id="S1.p8.13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, an operation <math id="S1.p8.14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> applied <em id="S1.p8.28.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">element-wise</em> is defined by <math id="S1.p8.15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{u}\circ\mathbf{v})_{i}=u_{i}\circ v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{u}\circ\mathbf{v})_{i}=u_{i}\circ v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p8.16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. For a modelling variable <math id="S1.p8.17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, its nominal value is <math id="S1.p8.18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}=\hat{X}-X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}=\hat{X}-X</annotation></semantics></math>. The Kronecker product is denoted by <math id="S1.p8.20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math>; let <math id="S1.p8.21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{x}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{x}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the element-wise absolute value. The inner product of vectors <math id="S1.p8.22.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by <math id="S1.p8.23.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. For a continuously differentiable convex function <math id="S1.p8.24.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a symmetric positive-definite matrix <math id="S1.p8.25.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the smooth projection operator is defined as <math id="S1.p8.26.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}(\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{y},f)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}-\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\frac{\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})(\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta}))^{\top}}{(\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta}))^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}f(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>𝚪</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}(\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{y},f)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}-\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\frac{\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})(\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta}))^{\top}}{(\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta}))^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}f(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p8.27.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\boldsymbol{\theta})&gt;0\land\mathbf{y}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\boldsymbol{\theta})&gt;0\land\mathbf{y}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{\theta})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.28.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}(\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{y},f)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>𝚪</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}(\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{y},f)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Formulation</span>
</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, consider a rigid <math id="S2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-DOF robotic manipulator with Euler-Lagrange dynamics</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{G}(\mathbf{q})+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)=\mathbf{u}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{G}(\mathbf{q})+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)=\mathbf{u}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.11" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}},\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}},\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the robot’s joint position, velocity, and acceleration vectors; <math id="S2.p1.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the positive-definite inertia matrix; <math id="S2.p1.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Coriolis matrix; <math id="S2.p1.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gravity torque vector; <math id="S2.p1.6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an unknown disturbance term (arising from unmodelled dynamics, friction, etc.); <math id="S2.p1.7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}\coloneqq\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}\coloneqq\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the human interaction torque, where <math id="S2.p1.8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is the end-effector wrench applied by the human and <math id="S2.p1.9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the manipulator’s Jacobian; <math id="S2.p1.10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the actuator torque. The wrench <math id="S2.p1.11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed measurable (e.g., via a wrist force–torque sensor).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following assumptions regarding the robotic manipulator and its task are made:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a bounded, continuously differentiable trajectory </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{z}_{d}(t),\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{d}(t),\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{d}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{z}_{d}(t),\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{d}(t),\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{d}(t))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in task space is predefined, where </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{d}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{d}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the smooth forward kinematics denoted </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{z}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{z}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and Jacobian denoted </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{6\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{6\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are known so that the actual end-effector pose satisfies </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{h}_{z}(\mathbf{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{h}_{z}(\mathbf{q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the disturbance </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is bounded and Lipschitz-continuous.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the true parameters are unknown, but identified nominal rigid-body parameters <math id="S2.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q}),\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}}),\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q}),\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}}),\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q}))</annotation></semantics></math> are available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="Dynamic Identification of the Franka Emika Panda Robot With Retrieval of Feasible Parameters Using Penalty-Based Optimization" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, giving the modelled dynamics</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})=\mathbf{u}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})=\mathbf{u}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.7" class="ltx_p">Consider the desired impedance model dynamics below</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{d}\ddot{\mathbf{e}}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)+\mathbf{B}_{d}\dot{\mathbf{e}}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)+\mathbf{K}_{d}\mathbf{e}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)=\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>imp</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>imp</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>imp</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{d}\ddot{\mathbf{e}}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)+\mathbf{B}_{d}\dot{\mathbf{e}}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)+\mathbf{K}_{d}\mathbf{e}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)=\mathbf{F}_{h}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)=\mathbf{z}_{d}(t)-\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>imp</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{z,\mathrm{imp}}(t)=\mathbf{z}_{d}(t)-\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a virtual task-space tracking error, <math id="S2.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{d},\ \mathbf{B}_{d},\ \mathbf{K}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{d},\ \mathbf{B}_{d},\ \mathbf{K}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive-definite designer matrices, and <math id="S2.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}},\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}},\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}})</annotation></semantics></math> solves (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). In general, <math id="S2.p3.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}\neq\mathbf{z}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}\neq\mathbf{z}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S2.p3.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. For notational simplicity, the independent variable <math id="S2.p3.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is dropped.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.11" class="ltx_p">For a generally defined control input <math id="S2.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full joint state and <math id="S2.p4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{z}=\mathbf{z}_{d}-\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{z}=\mathbf{z}_{d}-\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> the actual task-space tracking error. Let <math id="S2.p4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of joint states that satisfy the prescribed position and velocity limits. For control design, we select a compact convex subset <math id="S2.p4.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}\subset\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}\subset\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that defines the intended safe operating region. Define the admissible torque set <math id="S2.p4.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\coloneqq[\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min},\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\coloneqq[\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min},\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> and the barrier-free set <math id="S2.p4.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{b}\subset\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{b}\subset\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as a compact set strictly in the interior of <math id="S2.p4.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the CBF constraints are inactive. Let <math id="S2.p4.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the joint-space counterpart of <math id="S2.p4.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by kinematic inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, and let <math id="S2.p4.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{imp}}=(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}},\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{imp}}=(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}},\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\subset\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\subset\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of interaction wrenches for which hard satisfaction of both the state-safety and torque constraints is feasible at <math id="S2.p5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. A constructive characterization of <math id="S2.p5.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"><span class="ltx_text">III-D</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The control objective is to design a Lipschitz-continuous control law <math id="S2.p6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x},t)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for all <math id="S2.p6.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following statements hold:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for every initial condition <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the closed-loop system satisfies <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, the actuator torque satisfies <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}(t)\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}(t)\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all trajectories starting in <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the tracking error of the desired impedance trajectory <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly ultimately bounded.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S2.F1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:433.6pt;">
<foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.07em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;" height="0.7pt" width="1.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 1)" overflow="visible" class="ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2605.28367v2/" id="S2.F1.1.1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Block diagram of the proposed safety-critical online adaptive impedance controller. The architecture is colour-coded by functional module: grey blocks handle impedance trajectory generation and nominal acceleration computation via the impedance model, joint-space map, and PD law; blue blocks provide online adaptation and constraint-aware reference tracking via the IT2-FLS, disturbance observers, sliding mode compensator, adaptation laws, and command-driven reference model; the red block enforces state and torque constraints through the unified soft-constrained QP with embedded NCBFs and exact penalty functions; green blocks represent the physical robot and human interaction; and the purple block executes dynamic inversion for torque generation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methodology</span>
</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Impedance Trajectory Generation</span>
</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The impedance model (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is formulated in task space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the controller operates in joint space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We map task-space impedance acceleration to joint space via DLS; alternative mappings may be used without affecting the subsequent guarantees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> be the singular value decomposition of the Jacobian. Because <math id="S3.SS1.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;6</annotation></semantics></math>, near-singular configurations can arise, causing large joint velocities for small task-space commands. We employ an <em id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive damped least-squares</em> (DLS) inverse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="Robot Modeling and Control" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}=\mathbf{V}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{2}+\mathbf{K}_{\lambda})^{-1}\mathbf{U}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>DLS</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}=\mathbf{V}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{2}+\mathbf{K}_{\lambda})^{-1}\mathbf{U}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\lambda}=\operatorname{diag}(k_{\lambda,1},\dots,k_{\lambda,6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\lambda}=\operatorname{diag}(k_{\lambda,1},\dots,k_{\lambda,6})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\lambda,i}=k_{\max}\,g(\sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\lambda,i}=k_{\max}\,g(\sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma_{i})=2/(1+e^{\alpha_{s}\sigma_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma_{i})=2/(1+e^{\alpha_{s}\sigma_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sigmoid that scales the damping inversely with the singular value <math id="S3.SS1.p2.6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\max},\alpha_{s}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\max},\alpha_{s}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> are design parameters. The damping thus vanishes when the manipulator is far from a singularity and grows smoothly as any singular value decreases, bounding joint velocities without introducing discontinuities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">However, near a singularity, DLS alone can still produce a task-space acceleration that demands motion in nearly unreachable directions. To prevent this, we apply a <em id="S3.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">singularity-consistent</em> projection to the task-space impedance acceleration <math id="S3.SS1.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the weighting</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=\begin{cases}0,&amp;\sigma_{i}\geq\bar{\sigma},\\
\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\cos\!\left(\pi\tfrac{\sigma_{i}-\underline{\sigma}}{\bar{\sigma}-\underline{\sigma}}\right)\right),&amp;\underline{\sigma}&lt;\sigma_{i}&lt;\bar{\sigma},\\
1,&amp;\sigma_{i}\leq\underline{\sigma},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>cos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=\begin{cases}0,&amp;\sigma_{i}\geq\bar{\sigma},\\
\tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\cos\!\left(\pi\tfrac{\sigma_{i}-\underline{\sigma}}{\bar{\sigma}-\underline{\sigma}}\right)\right),&amp;\underline{\sigma}&lt;\sigma_{i}&lt;\bar{\sigma},\\
1,&amp;\sigma_{i}\leq\underline{\sigma},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\sigma}&lt;\bar{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\sigma}&lt;\bar{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> are activation thresholds. The singularity-consistent acceleration is then <math id="S3.SS1.p3.6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\ddot{\mathbf{z}}}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\sum_{i}w_{i}(\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\top}\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}})\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\ddot{\mathbf{z}}}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\sum_{i}w_{i}(\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\top}\ddot{\mathbf{z}}_{\mathrm{imp}})\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which smoothly zeroes acceleration components in near-singular task-space directions, preventing discontinuous jumps in the joint-space reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">To utilise the redundancy of the manipulator for energy dissipation, we add a null-space damping term. The resulting impedance joint acceleration is</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\!\left(\bar{\ddot{\mathbf{z}}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\dot{\mathbf{J}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>DLS</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\!\left(\bar{\ddot{\mathbf{z}}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\dot{\mathbf{J}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-(\mathbf{I}_{n}-\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\mathbf{J})(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{damp}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>DLS</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>damp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-(\mathbf{I}_{n}-\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\mathbf{J})(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{damp}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{damp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>damp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{damp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive-definite damping gain. The null-space projector <math id="S3.SS1.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{I}_{n}-\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\mathbf{J})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>DLS</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{I}_{n}-\mathbf{J}^{\dagger}_{\mathrm{DLS}}\mathbf{J})</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the joint damping does not interfere with the task-space impedance behaviour.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design</span>
</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The NCBFs below define hard acceleration bounds in the unified QP of Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"><span class="ltx_text">III-D</span></span></a>. A command-driven reference model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="Stable Adaptive Systems" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> tracks the QP output <math id="S3.SS2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{ref}}=(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}},\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{ref}}=(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}},\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> with double-integrator dynamics</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}&amp;\mathbf{I}_{n}\\
\mathbf{0}&amp;\mathbf{0}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{ref}}+\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{I}_{n}\end{bmatrix}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{u}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>𝟎</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}&amp;\mathbf{I}_{n}\\
\mathbf{0}&amp;\mathbf{0}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{ref}}+\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{0}\\
\mathbf{I}_{n}\end{bmatrix}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{u}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{u}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\mathbf{K}_{v}(\dot{\mathbf{q}}-\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{ref}})+\mathbf{K}_{p}(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{u}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\mathbf{K}_{v}(\dot{\mathbf{q}}-\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{ref}})+\mathbf{K}_{p}(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{v},\mathbf{K}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{v},\mathbf{K}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive-definite diagonal matrices satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.p1.5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{v}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}+\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{v}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}+\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{p}=\mathbf{K}_{r}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{p}=\mathbf{K}_{r}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{\Lambda},\mathbf{K}_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{\Lambda},\mathbf{K}_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"><span class="ltx_text">III-C</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}\coloneqq[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}\coloneqq[n]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of joint indices. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denote joints subject only to velocity constraints, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> those subject to both position and velocity constraints (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}=\mathcal{J}_{v}\cup\mathcal{J}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}=\mathcal{J}_{v}\cup\mathcal{J}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>). The physical limit set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}:(|\dot{q}_{i}|\leq\dot{q}_{\max,i}\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J})\;\land(|q_{i}|\leq q_{\max,i}\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}:(|\dot{q}_{i}|\leq\dot{q}_{\max,i}\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J})\;\land(|q_{i}|\leq q_{\max,i}\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p})\}</annotation></semantics></math> was introduced in Section <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>; the designer selects a compact convex subset <math id="S3.SS2.p2.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}\subset\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}\subset\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as the intended operating region.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Velocity-only joints (<math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{J}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{J}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">A valid NCBF for the velocity constraint set is</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{j\in[2]}{\{{{}^{i}}h_{vj}(\mathbf{x})\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{j\in[2]}{\{{{}^{i}}h_{vj}(\mathbf{x})\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{v1}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\dot{q}_{\max,i}-x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{v1}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\dot{q}_{\max,i}-x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{v2}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\dot{q}_{\max,i}+x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{v2}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\dot{q}_{\max,i}+x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the left superscript <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the barrier function for the <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th joint, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are its position and velocity states.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A function is <em id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Clarke regular</em> at a point if its generalised directional derivative coincides with the one-sided directional derivative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. The function (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In Velocity-only joints (𝑖∈𝒥_𝑣) ‣ III-B Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) is not Clarke regular due to its boolean composition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. As <math id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz, but not Clarke regular, we invoke the weak set-valued Lie derivative result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> for the NCBF.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Position-and-velocity joints (<math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Composing <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{vj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{vj}</annotation></semantics></math> naively with position barriers directly yields a relative-degree-2 constraint, making the CBF-Quadratic Programme (CBF-QP) solution non-Lipschitz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="Control Barrier Functions: Theory and Applications" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="Is Your Safe Controller Actually Safe? A Critical Review of CBF Tautologies and Hidden Assumptions" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Instead, we propose the NCBF composition:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{j\in[2]}{\{{{}^{i}}h_{pj}(\mathbf{x})\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{j\in[2]}{\{{{}^{i}}h_{pj}(\mathbf{x})\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{p1}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq 1-R_{1}^{l}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{1}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-R_{1}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{p1}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq 1-R_{1}^{l}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{1}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-R_{1}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{p2}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq 1-R_{2}^{l}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{1}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-R_{2}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{p2}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq 1-R_{2}^{l}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{1}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-R_{2}\!\left(\tfrac{x_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}(\cdot)\coloneqq\max\{0,(\cdot)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}(\cdot)\coloneqq\max\{0,(\cdot)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}(\cdot)\coloneqq\max\{0,-(\cdot)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}(\cdot)\coloneqq\max\{0,-(\cdot)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is an even integer. Similar to <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{}^{i}}h_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{}^{i}}h_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz but not Clarke regular.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.10" class="ltx_p">Applying the weak set-valued Lie derivative from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and Brezis’ Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="On a characterization of flow‐invariant sets" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, the NCBF conditions</p>
<table id="A0.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min{\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))}\geq-\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{v}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min{\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))}\geq-\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min{\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))}\geq-\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min{\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))}\geq-\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> are tuning parameters controlling the convergence rate to the constraint boundary (larger <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> allows faster approach, but requires larger torque), and</p>
<table id="A0.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}(h(\mathbf{x}))\coloneqq\{a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{F}^{W}(h(\mathbf{x}))\coloneqq\{a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:\exists\ \mathbf{v}\in\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}),\ \boldsymbol{\zeta}\in\partial{h(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">∃</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:\exists\ \mathbf{v}\in\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}),\ \boldsymbol{\zeta}\in\partial{h(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ s.t.\ }\langle\boldsymbol{\zeta},\mathbf{v}\rangle=a\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t. </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ s.t.\ }\langle\boldsymbol{\zeta},\mathbf{v}\rangle=a\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.9" class="ltx_p">denotes the weak set-valued Lie derivative, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial h</annotation></semantics></math> the Clarke generalised gradient of <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> the flow of the closed-loop vector field, translate into the element-wise acceleration bounds <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{l}=l-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{l}=l-1</annotation></semantics></math>. For velocity-only joints <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{A,i},\alpha_{B,i})=(\alpha_{vA,i},\alpha_{vB,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{A,i},\alpha_{B,i})=(\alpha_{vA,i},\alpha_{vB,i})</annotation></semantics></math> and for position-and-velocity joints <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{A,i},\alpha_{B,i})=(\alpha_{pA,i},\alpha_{pB,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{A,i},\alpha_{B,i})=(\alpha_{pA,i},\alpha_{pB,i})</annotation></semantics></math>, with</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{vA,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{vA,i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3X.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v2}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\alpha_{vB,i}=\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v1}(\mathbf{x}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v2}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\quad\alpha_{vB,i}=\gamma_{v}{{}^{i}}h_{v1}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{pA,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{pA,i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xa.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left(-l\frac{\dot{q}_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}R_{2}^{\bar{l}}\!\left(\tfrac{q_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p2}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left(-l\frac{\dot{q}_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}R_{2}^{\bar{l}}\!\left(\tfrac{q_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)-\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p2}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xb.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{pB,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{pB,i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3Xb.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left(-l\frac{\dot{q}_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}R_{1}^{\bar{l}}\!\left(\tfrac{q_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)+\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p1}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left(-l\frac{\dot{q}_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}R_{1}^{\bar{l}}\!\left(\tfrac{q_{i}}{q_{\max,i}}\right)+\gamma_{p}{{}^{i}}h_{p1}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p2.11" class="ltx_p">These bounds are enforced as hard constraints in the QP (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.6" class="ltx_p">Define the NCBF super-level set <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}:{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\geq 0,\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{v};\;{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\geq 0,\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}:{{}^{i}}h_{v}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\geq 0,\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{v};\;{{}^{i}}h_{p}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\geq 0,\,\forall i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}\subseteq\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}\subseteq\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> for suitably chosen <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(l,\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p},\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(l,\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p},\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>. On <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A,i}\leq 0\leq\alpha_{B,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A,i}\leq 0\leq\alpha_{B,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p3.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design</span>
</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S3.SS3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In III-B Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), define the reference-model tracking error <math id="S3.SS3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}(t)=\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)-\mathbf{q}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}(t)=\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}(t)-\mathbf{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the filtered reference-model tracking error <math id="S3.SS3.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}(t)=\dot{\mathbf{e}}(t)+\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{e}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}(t)=\dot{\mathbf{e}}(t)+\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{e}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Lambda},\mathbf{K}_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Lambda},\mathbf{K}_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive-definite diagonal matrices. Because <math id="S3.SS3.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is driven by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects mismatch between the plant and the command-driven reference model, not constraint enforcement or impedance-model tracking error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let the nominal control torque be</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{\mathrm{nom}}=\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>nom</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{\mathrm{nom}}=\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) below. Substituting (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) into (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) gives the dynamics</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\mathbf{M}^{-1}\!\left(\tilde{\mathbf{M}}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\tilde{\mathbf{C}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\tilde{\mathbf{G}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\mathbf{M}^{-1}\!\left(\tilde{\mathbf{M}}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\tilde{\mathbf{C}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\tilde{\mathbf{G}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Design <math id="S3.SS3.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}</annotation></semantics></math> to stabilise the tracking error in the presence of the unknown disturbance term and model mismatch:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\hat{\mathbf{f}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\hat{\mathbf{f}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the commanded acceleration from the QP (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), <math id="S3.SS3.p3.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is an IT2-FLS approximator, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) is a DOB-based compensator, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) is an adaptive sliding-mode compensator (SMC).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{\xi}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{\xi}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) into (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), one obtains</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\boldsymbol{\delta}(t),\quad\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>des</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{des}}+\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\boldsymbol{\delta}(t),\quad\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})=\mathbf{M}^{-1}\!\left(\tilde{\mathbf{M}}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c})+\tilde{\mathbf{C}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\tilde{\mathbf{G}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})=\mathbf{M}^{-1}\!\left(\tilde{\mathbf{M}}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c})+\tilde{\mathbf{C}}\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\tilde{\mathbf{G}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the structured uncertainty representing model mismatch, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}=(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}},\bar{\boldsymbol{\xi}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}=(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}},\bar{\boldsymbol{\xi}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\delta}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\delta}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unstructured residual.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{3n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{3n}</annotation></semantics></math> is compact. Let <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}\coloneqq\mathbf{S}_{i}\boldsymbol{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛏</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}\coloneqq\mathbf{S}_{i}\boldsymbol{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3n}</annotation></semantics></math> the selection matrix for the <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.5.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th joint, and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.6.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{i}\coloneqq\mathbf{S}_{i}(\Omega)\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{i}\coloneqq\mathbf{S}_{i}(\Omega)\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.7.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.8.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\Omega_{i}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\Omega_{i}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a residual <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.9.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})+\mathbf{g}(\boldsymbol{\xi})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})+\mathbf{g}(\boldsymbol{\xi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.10.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\coloneqq(f_{1},\dots,f_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\coloneqq(f_{1},\dots,f_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, by the universal approximation theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="Fuzzy basis functions, universal approximation, and orthogonal least-squares learning" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by a set of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> IT2-TSK-FLSs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="Fuzzy Control and Modeling: Analytical Foundations and Applications" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="Modern Adaptive Fuzzy Control Systems" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})=\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\theta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})=\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\theta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.12" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the regressor containing normalised firing strengths from the type-reduced FLS output, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the consequent weight vector, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=m\cdot n\cdot M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=m\cdot n\cdot M</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p5.6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> consequent parameters per rule, and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> rules. The IT2-FLS uses Gaussian interval-type-2 membership functions; for computational tractability, type-reduction is performed using the Nie-Tan operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="Modern Adaptive Fuzzy Control Systems" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. The FLS inputs are chosen as <math id="S3.SS3.p5.8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}=(\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i}),\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}=(\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i}),\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\xi}_{i}:=\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{c,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\xi}_{i}:=\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{c,i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <math id="S3.SS3.p5.10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S3.SS3.p5.11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> directly ensures all fuzzy inputs remain Lipschitz, guaranteeing the compactness of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Define the ideal weights</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\sup_{\boldsymbol{\xi}\in\Omega}\left\|\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})\right\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}=\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\sup_{\boldsymbol{\xi}\in\Omega}\left\|\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">and the disturbance term</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}(t)=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*})-\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t),\quad\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}(t)=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*})-\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t),\quad\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\delta}(t)-\mathbf{g}(\boldsymbol{\xi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t)\coloneqq\boldsymbol{\delta}(t)-\mathbf{g}(\boldsymbol{\xi})</annotation></semantics></math> is a modified residual. The DOB-CBF disturbance is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})-\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t)-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>CBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)=\mathbf{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta})-\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}(t)-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.10.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the disturbances <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}(t),\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>CBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}(t),\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and their derivatives <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{d}}(t),\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>CBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{d}}(t),\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded with known boundaries: <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{d}(t)\|\leq\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{d}(t)\|\leq\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.5.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>CBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.6.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}(t)\|\leq\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}(t)\|\leq\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.7.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{1},\ \forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>CBF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(t)\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{1},\ \forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.8.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0},\bar{\omega}_{0},\omega_{1},\bar{\omega}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0},\bar{\omega}_{0},\omega_{1},\bar{\omega}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, the ideal weights are bounded by a known positive constant: <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.9.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}\|\leq\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{\theta}^{*}\|\leq\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.10.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The existence of these bounds follows from the compactness of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the Lipschitz continuity of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}(\boldsymbol{\xi})</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), and standard DOB theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Their exact values need not be known; instead, conservative bounds suffice.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the dynamics in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we adopt the DOBs of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, with the form</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}=\boldsymbol{\ell}_{c}+\alpha_{c}\dot{\mathbf{q}},\quad\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}=\boldsymbol{\ell}_{o}+\alpha_{o}\dot{\mathbf{q}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}=\boldsymbol{\ell}_{c}+\alpha_{c}\dot{\mathbf{q}},\quad\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}=\boldsymbol{\ell}_{o}+\alpha_{o}\dot{\mathbf{q}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p7.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c},\alpha_{o}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c},\alpha_{o}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> are design parameters. The observer states evolve according to</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\boldsymbol{\ell}}_{c}=-\alpha_{c}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}),\quad\dot{\boldsymbol{\ell}}_{o}=-\alpha_{o}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}+\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\boldsymbol{\ell}}_{c}=-\alpha_{c}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}),\quad\dot{\boldsymbol{\ell}}_{o}=-\alpha_{o}(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}+\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.7" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS3.p8.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates the residual disturbance after the learning loop, while <math id="S3.SS3.p8.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> estimates the total uncertainty in the system (used for the CBF safety analysis). Define the estimation error of the compensation DOB as <math id="S3.SS3.p8.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{d}:=\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\mathbf{d}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{d}:=\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\mathbf{d}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and following the analysis in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Sec. II-B]</cite>, <math id="S3.SS3.p8.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly ultimately bounded. We define this ultimate bound as follows: <math id="S3.SS3.p8.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}=\sup|\mathbf{e}_{d}(t)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}=\sup|\mathbf{e}_{d}(t)|</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS3.p8.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{w}\right\|\leq W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{w}\right\|\leq W</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS3.p8.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation</span>
</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The NCBF acceleration bounds from Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"><span class="ltx_text">III-B</span></span></a> guarantee state safety when enforced, but do not account for actuator torque limits <math id="S3.SS4.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. When both CBF and torque constraints are imposed simultaneously, the resulting hard-constrained QP can become infeasible, e.g. during large interaction wrenches or severe model mismatch. We resolve this by embedding both constraint types in a single unified QP, <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">softening</em> only the torque constraints with an exact penalty function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="Soft constraints and exact penalty functions in model predictive control" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, while keeping the CBF constraints hard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.SS4.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{d}(0)=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{d}(0)=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (achievable by initialising (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) offline). Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in(0,2\alpha_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in(0,2\alpha_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\}\in(0,2\alpha_{o}-\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\}\in(0,2\alpha_{o}-\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the robust safety margin</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}\coloneqq\begin{cases}\dfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}}{2\nu\beta}+\dfrac{\beta}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{v})},&amp;i\in\mathcal{J}_{v},\\[6.0pt]
\dfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{2\nu\beta}+\dfrac{\beta/\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{p})},&amp;i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}\coloneqq\begin{cases}\dfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}}{2\nu\beta}+\dfrac{\beta}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{v})},&amp;i\in\mathcal{J}_{v},\\[6.0pt]
\dfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{2\nu\beta}+\dfrac{\beta/\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{p})},&amp;i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.7" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},A,i}\coloneqq-\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{o,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},A,i}\coloneqq-\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{o,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}</annotation></semantics></math> (lower-bound direction) and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},B,i}\coloneqq-\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{o,i}-\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},B,i}\coloneqq-\ddot{q}_{s,i}-\hat{d}_{o,i}-\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}</annotation></semantics></math> (upper-bound direction). Define the base torque <math id="S3.SS4.p2.7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}=\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}-\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}-\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})-\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}=\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h}-\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}}-\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\mathbf{q})-\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}-\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c}-\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\boldsymbol{\xi};\boldsymbol{\theta}))</annotation></semantics></math>, which collects the known dynamic terms and robust compensators. The nominal acceleration couples the impedance model to the plant state via</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}+\mathbf{K}_{v}(\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\dot{\mathbf{q}})+\mathbf{K}_{p}(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\mathbf{q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>nom</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}+\mathbf{K}_{v}(\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\dot{\mathbf{q}})+\mathbf{K}_{p}(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\mathbf{q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.8" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S3.SS4.p2.8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{QP}}\coloneqq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}-\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>QP</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>nom</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{QP}}\coloneqq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}-\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The commanded acceleration solves</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E22.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center" colspan="2"><math id="S3.E22.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}},\mathbf{s}^{*})=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\ddot{\mathbf{q}},\,\mathbf{s}}\;\tfrac{1}{2}\|\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}\|^{2}+\rho\,\mathbf{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}+\tfrac{\varepsilon_{s}}{2}\|\mathbf{s}\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>nom</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐬</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}},\mathbf{s}^{*})=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\ddot{\mathbf{q}},\,\mathbf{s}}\;\tfrac{1}{2}\|\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}\|^{2}+\rho\,\mathbf{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}+\tfrac{\varepsilon_{s}}{2}\|\mathbf{s}\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22a)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="A0.EGx3" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E22.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E22.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},B}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22b)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E22.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}-\mathbf{s}\leq\hat{\mathbf{M}}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}+\mathbf{s}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐬</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>base</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐬</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}-\mathbf{s}\leq\hat{\mathbf{M}}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}+\mathbf{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22c)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E22.4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{s}\geq\mathbf{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{s}\geq\mathbf{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22d)</span></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.11" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> relaxes the torque constraints, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the exact penalty weight, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> regularises the slack. The CBF constraints (<a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a>) remain hard; only (<a href="#S3.E22.3" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22c</span></a>) is softened.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">For sufficiently large <math id="S3.SS4.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="Soft constraints and exact penalty functions in model predictive control" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}^{*}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}^{*}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the hard-constrained problem is feasible. Otherwise, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> absorbs the minimum torque violation while (<a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a>) stays active.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The admissible wrench set <math id="S3.SS4.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\subset\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\subset\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S2" title="II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> is</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E23X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\big\{\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}:\exists\,\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\text{ s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mtext> s.t.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\big\{\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}:\exists\,\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\text{ s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E23Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\eqref{eq: robust_CBF},\ \eqref{eq: torque_bounds},\ \mathbf{s}=\mathbf{0}\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.E22.3" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22c</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eqref{eq: robust_CBF},\ \eqref{eq: torque_bounds},\ \mathbf{s}=\mathbf{0}\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The control torque is</p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>base</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which reduces to the nominal control (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) when <math id="S3.SS4.p4.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>nom</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}=\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{nom}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., no constraints active).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Feasibility Metric</span>
</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.5" class="ltx_p">To monitor proximity to torque infeasibility online, define for each unit direction <math id="S3.SS5.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}\coloneqq\{\pm\mathbf{e}_{1},\pm\mathbf{e}_{2},\pm\mathbf{e}_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}\coloneqq\{\pm\mathbf{e}_{1},\pm\mathbf{e}_{2},\pm\mathbf{e}_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> the largest wrench magnitude <math id="S3.SS5.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with (<a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a>) and zero-slack torque feasibility. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>base</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote <math id="S3.SS5.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the interaction wrench removed. For <math id="S3.SS5.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}=F\mathbf{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐝</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}=F\mathbf{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}\leq\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}+F\,\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>base</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐝</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}\leq\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}+F\,\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.9" class="ltx_p">For fixed <math id="S3.SS5.p1.6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and direction <math id="S3.SS5.p1.7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the largest magnitude <math id="S3.SS5.p1.8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> for which there exists an acceleration <math id="S3.SS5.p1.9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying both (<a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In III-E Feasibility Metric ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) is obtained by the linear programme</p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E26.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center" colspan="2"><math id="S3.E26.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x})=\max_{F,\,\ddot{\mathbf{q}}}\;F" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x})=\max_{F,\,\ddot{\mathbf{q}}}\;F</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26a)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="A0.EGx4" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E26.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E26.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s.t.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},B}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},A}\leq\ddot{\mathbf{q}}\leq\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{rob},B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26b)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E26.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\,F\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>base</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}-\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\,F\leq\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}+\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26c)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E26.4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\,F\leq-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mrow><mi>base</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})^{\top}\mathbf{d}\,F\leq-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min}-\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{base},0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26d)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S3.E26.5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26e)</span></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.10" class="ltx_p">Problem (<a href="#S3.E26" title="In III-E Feasibility Metric ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) is evaluated for each <math id="S3.SS5.p1.10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the online metric is</p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}}F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}(\mathbf{x})\coloneqq\min_{\mathbf{d}\in\mathcal{D}}F_{\mathbf{d}}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.12" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.SS5.p1.11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\|&gt;F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\|&gt;F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, zero-slack torque feasibility is lost and the soft-constrained QP activates <math id="S3.SS5.p1.12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}^{*}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}^{*}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Main Results</span>
</h2>

<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a bounded, Lipschitz continuous reference <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto(\mathbf{q}_{r}(t),\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}(t),\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto(\mathbf{q}_{r}(t),\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}(t),\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\coloneqq\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\coloneqq\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Jacobian. Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.5.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact set and the functions <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.6.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}},\mathbf{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}},\mathbf{J}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth maps. Then <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.7.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\ c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">∃</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\ c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.8.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q}_{r})-\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q})\right\|\leq c_{1}\left\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{q}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q}_{r})-\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{z}}(\mathbf{q})\right\|\leq c_{1}\left\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{q}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.9.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q}_{r})\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}-\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})\dot{\mathbf{q}}\right\|\leq c_{2}\left\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{q}\right\|+c_{3}\left\|\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}-\dot{\mathbf{q}}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q}_{r})\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}-\mathbf{J}(\mathbf{q})\dot{\mathbf{q}}\right\|\leq c_{2}\left\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{q}\right\|+c_{3}\left\|\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{r}-\dot{\mathbf{q}}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof:</h6>
<div id="S4.1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Follows from Lipschitz Continuity and the Mean Value Theorem. Refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="Nonlinear Systems" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="A robust adaptive nonlinear control design" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>)</span>
</h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R},\ b\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R},\ b\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\approx 0.2785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.2785</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\approx 0.2785</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa+\ln(\kappa)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa+\ln(\kappa)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.5.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq|a|b-ab\tanh{\left(\kappa\frac{ab}{\varepsilon}\right)}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>ε</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq|a|b-ab\tanh{\left(\kappa\frac{ab}{\varepsilon}\right)}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="Disturbance Observer-based Robust Control Barrier Functions" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>)</span>
</h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist positive constants <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p},\,\gamma_{v},\,\alpha_{o},\,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p},\,\gamma_{v},\,\alpha_{o},\,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}}\Big[\mathcal{L}_{f}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})-\|\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})\|\mathcal{W}-\tfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}}{2\nu\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}}\Big[\mathcal{L}_{f}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})-\|\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})\|\mathcal{W}-\tfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}}{2\nu\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex1Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\tfrac{\beta\|\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})\|^{2}}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{(\cdot)})}+\gamma_{(\cdot)}{{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\tfrac{\beta\|\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})\|^{2}}{4\alpha_{o}-2(\nu+\gamma_{(\cdot)})}+\gamma_{(\cdot)}{{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex1Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1Xb.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{s}})\Big]\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}({{}^{i}}h_{(\cdot)})(\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}+\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{s}})\Big]\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{f}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{f}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{g}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math> are the weak set-valued Lie derivatives of the closed-loop system, and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\bar{\omega}_{0}+\sqrt{\bar{\omega}_{0}^{2}+\tfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}}{\nu(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\bar{\omega}_{0}+\sqrt{\bar{\omega}_{0}^{2}+\tfrac{\bar{\omega}_{1}}{\nu(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E24" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 ‣ II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 ([28]) ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. For all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, let the update laws with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-Projection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="Projection Operator in Adaptive Systems" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-modification be</span></p>
<table id="A0.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}}\!\big(\boldsymbol{\theta}(t),-\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\mathbf{r}(t),f(\boldsymbol{\theta})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Proj}_{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}}\!\big(\boldsymbol{\theta}(t),-\boldsymbol{\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\mathbf{r}(t),f(\boldsymbol{\theta})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\hat{\mathbf{w}}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\hat{\mathbf{w}}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}|\mathbf{r}(t)|-\sigma_{w}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}\hat{\mathbf{w}}(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}|\mathbf{r}(t)|-\sigma_{w}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}\hat{\mathbf{w}}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}=\operatorname{diag}(\gamma_{\theta 1},\dots,\gamma_{\theta n})\otimes\mathbf{I}_{m\cdot M}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}=\operatorname{diag}(\gamma_{\theta 1},\dots,\gamma_{\theta n})\otimes\mathbf{I}_{m\cdot M}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}=\operatorname{diag}(\gamma_{w1},\dots,\gamma_{wn})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}=\operatorname{diag}(\gamma_{w1},\dots,\gamma_{wn})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive-definite gains, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.6.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a small leakage parameter, and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.7.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\tfrac{\left\|\boldsymbol{\theta}\right\|^{2}-\Theta^{2}}{2\varepsilon\Theta+\varepsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\tfrac{\left\|\boldsymbol{\theta}\right\|^{2}-\Theta^{2}}{2\varepsilon\Theta+\varepsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex bounding function with nominal bound <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.8.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and tolerance <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.9.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the DOB updates be as in (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), and define the SMC term be</span></p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{si}=\hat{w}_{i}\tanh{\!\left(\frac{\kappa}{\varepsilon_{i}}r_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{si}=\hat{w}_{i}\tanh{\!\left(\frac{\kappa}{\varepsilon_{i}}r_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.14.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.10.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.11.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0},\ (\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}\coloneqq\{(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n+6}:\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0},\ (\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}\coloneqq\{(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n+6}:\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.12.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.13.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n+6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n+6}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of the wrench <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.14.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the following statements hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U},\,\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U},\,\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}^{*},\,\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}^{*},\,\forall t\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tracking error of the desired impedance trajectory </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is uniformly ultimately bounded.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_proof"><span id="S4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span></h6>
<div id="S4.2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">See Appendix <a href="#A0.SS1" title="-A Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"><span class="ltx_text">-A</span></span></a>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dropping the leakage term (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) and replacing (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) with a discontinuous SMC yields asymptotic stability of the impedance tracking error (when <math id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)\in\mathcal{X}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)\in\mathcal{X}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and the torque constraints are inactive), but Filippov solutions and a redefinition of the NCBFs would be required. In addition, the discontinuous nature of the new control law induces high-frequency oscillations in the control signal, leading to chattering, which can damage the actuators.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Forward invariance via Glotfelter’s NCBF theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="Nonsmooth Barrier Functions With Applications to Multi-Robot Systems" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> requires the control law to admit at least Carathéodory solutions, which presupposes local Lipschitz continuity. Because the QP (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) involves the state-dependent inertia <math id="S4.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})</annotation></semantics></math> in its constraint matrix, the standard piecewise-affine conclusions for multiparametric QPs do not apply directly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="Reformulations of Quadratic Programs for Lipschitz Continuity" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The following proposition confirms that the QP solution retains the required regularity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be compact and chosen to exclude kinematic singularities, and let <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(\ddot{\mathbf{q}},\mathbf{s})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(\ddot{\mathbf{q}},\mathbf{s})\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the decision vector of the QP (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). Write the QP in parametric form as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\mathbf{z}}\;\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{z}^{\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{z}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{z}\leq\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐳</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐇𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">s.t.</mtext></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\mathbf{z}}\;\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{z}^{\top}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{z}+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{x})^{\top}\mathbf{z}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{z}\leq\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=\operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{I}_{n},\,\varepsilon_{s}\mathbf{I}_{n})\succ\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≻</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=\operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{I}_{n},\,\varepsilon_{s}\mathbf{I}_{n})\succ\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the constant Hessian, and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> collect the state-dependent QP data. Suppose the following hold on <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.5.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(P1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Uniform strong convexity) </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}\succeq\mu\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}\succeq\mu\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(P2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Lipschitz data) </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}(\cdot),\mathbf{b}(\cdot),\mathbf{h}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}(\cdot),\mathbf{b}(\cdot),\mathbf{h}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are Lipschitz on </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(P3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Uniform feasibility) The feasible set </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{z}:\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{z}\leq\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x})\}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{z}:\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{z}\leq\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x})\}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},A,i}&lt;\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},B,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>rob</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},A,i}&lt;\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B,i}+\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{rob},B,i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> element-wise.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(P4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(MFCQ) The Mangasarian–Fromovitz constraint qualification </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="Mangasarian-Fromovitz-type constraint qualification and optimality conditions for smooth infinite programming problems" class="ltx_ref">3</a><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds at </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the commanded acceleration <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.7.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, are locally Lipschitz on <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.8.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof:</h6>
<div id="S4.3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Uniform strong convexity <a href="#S4.I2.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds by construction since <math id="S4.3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Local Lipschitz continuity of <math id="S4.3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> then follows from classical sensitivity theory for strongly convex parametric optimisation problems (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="Perturbation Analysis of Optimization Problems" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>). Since <math id="S4.3.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}=[\mathbf{I}_{n}\;\;\mathbf{0}]\mathbf{z}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">  0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}=[\mathbf{I}_{n}\;\;\mathbf{0}]\mathbf{z}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the result extends to <math id="S4.3.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math> by linearity.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verification of Lipschitz Continuity</span>
</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We verify that the control law (<a href="#S3.E24" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) constructed in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> satisfies Assumptions <a href="#S4.I2.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.I2.i4" title="item 4 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on the compact operating set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>Lipschitz Data (Assumption <a href="#S4.I2.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">For the constraint data <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to be Lipschitz on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, each constituent function forming the bounds <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{lower}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>lower</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{lower}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{upper}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>upper</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{upper}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> must itself be locally Lipschitz. We verify this for each component:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">IT2-FLS (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> The approximator uses Gaussian membership functions and the Nie-Tan type-reduction operator, both <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to their inputs. The FLS inputs <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}=(\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i}),\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}_{i}=(\sin(q_{i}),\cos(q_{i}),\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth bounded mappings of the state, so <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{f}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SMC (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> The smooth compensator <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{si}=\hat{w}_{i}\tanh(\kappa r_{i}\hat{w}_{i}/\varepsilon_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{si}=\hat{w}_{i}\tanh(\kappa r_{i}\hat{w}_{i}/\varepsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and globally Lipschitz, by design avoiding the standard discontinuous signum function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DOB (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c},\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{c},\hat{\mathbf{d}}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> The observers (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) are affine in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> with smooth internal states, hence locally Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.6" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NCBF bounds (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A},\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛂</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛂</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A},\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> These are composed using <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}(\cdot)=\max\{0,\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}(\cdot)=\max\{0,\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}(\cdot)=\max\{0,-(\cdot)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}(\cdot)=\max\{0,-(\cdot)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are globally Lipschitz with constant <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inertia (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> excludes kinematic singularities, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{M}}(\mathbf{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and strictly positive-definite on an open neighbourhood of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence Lipschitz on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>Uniform Feasibility (Assumption <a href="#S4.I2.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Because the slack variables <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> can absorb arbitrary torque-constraint violations, uniform feasibility of the QP reduces to showing that the hard CBF acceleration box remains strictly non-degenerate: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{lower},i}(\mathbf{x})&lt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{upper},i}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>lower</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>upper</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{lower},i}(\mathbf{x})&lt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{upper},i}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the width <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\ddot{q}_{i}\coloneqq(\alpha_{B,i}-\alpha_{A,i})-2\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\ddot{q}_{i}\coloneqq(\alpha_{B,i}-\alpha_{A,i})-2\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This condition concerns state-side feasibility only; torque infeasibility under large interaction wrenches is handled separately by the slack variables and is the subject of the infeasibility simulation in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Velocity constraints (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{J}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{J}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> The CBF bounds yield the constant width <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{vB,i}-\alpha_{vA,i}=2\gamma_{v}\dot{q}_{\max,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{vB,i}-\alpha_{vA,i}=2\gamma_{v}\dot{q}_{\max,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the non-degeneracy condition is</p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}\dot{q}_{\max,i}&gt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},v,i}(\gamma_{v})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}\dot{q}_{\max,i}&gt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},v,i}(\gamma_{v}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Position constraints (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{J}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>):</em> The width varies with the state. Exploiting the geometry of the composed NCBFs—specifically that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{p1}+h_{p2}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{p1}+h_{p2}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>—and evaluating at the critical boundary yields the analytical minimum:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{pB,i}-\alpha_{pA,i}\geq\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left[\gamma_{p}-l\frac{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{q_{\max,i}}K(l)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{pB,i}-\alpha_{pA,i}\geq\dot{q}_{\max,i}\!\left[\gamma_{p}-l\frac{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}{q_{\max,i}}K(l)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(l)=\left(\tfrac{l-1}{2l-1}\right)^{\frac{l-1}{l}}\left(\tfrac{l}{2l-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>l</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(l)=\left(\tfrac{l-1}{2l-1}\right)^{\frac{l-1}{l}}\left(\tfrac{l}{2l-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The non-degeneracy condition becomes</p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}\dot{q}_{\max,i}&gt;2\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},p,i}(\gamma_{p})+l\frac{\dot{q}_{\max,i}^{2}}{q_{\max,i}}K(l)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}\dot{q}_{\max,i}&gt;2\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},p,i}(\gamma_{p})+l\frac{\dot{q}_{\max,i}^{2}}{q_{\max,i}}K(l).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.7.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Parameter feasibility:</em> The robust margin <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is not static; it depends on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg}}(\gamma)=C_{1}+C_{2}/(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>marg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg}}(\gamma)=C_{1}+C_{2}/(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}/(2\nu\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=\bar{\omega}_{1}^{2}/(2\nu\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=\beta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=\beta/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg}}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>marg</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg}}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>, conditions (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E32" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) define coupled inequalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Velocity case:</em> Multiplying (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) by the positive factor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> yields the quadratic:</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E33X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E33X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{q}_{\max,i}\gamma_{v}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{q}_{\max,i}\gamma_{v}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E33X.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\!\left(\dot{q}_{\max,i}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+C_{1}\right)\gamma_{v}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\!\left(\dot{q}_{\max,i}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+C_{1}\right)\gamma_{v}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S4.E33Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E33Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\left(C_{1}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+C_{2}\right)&lt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\left(C_{1}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+C_{2}\right)&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">A feasible tuning interval <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}\in(\gamma_{v,\min},\gamma_{v,\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}\in(\gamma_{v,\min},\gamma_{v,\max})</annotation></semantics></math> exists if and only if the discriminant of (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) is strictly positive, which holds when the physical joint kinematic limits <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}_{\max,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}_{\max,i}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently outweigh the worst-case disturbance bounds <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>—a condition met by appropriately scaled robotic hardware and observer gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Position case:</em> Define <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}\coloneqq l\,\dot{q}_{\max,i}^{2}\,K(l)/q_{\max,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}\coloneqq l\,\dot{q}_{\max,i}^{2}\,K(l)/q_{\max,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{C}_{1}\coloneqq 2C_{1}\dot{q}_{\max,i}+C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{C}_{1}\coloneqq 2C_{1}\dot{q}_{\max,i}+C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the factor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2C_{1}\dot{q}_{\max,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2C_{1}\dot{q}_{\max,i}</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}_{\max,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}_{\max,i}</annotation></semantics></math>-scaling of the robust margin in the position case, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the state-dependent NCBF curvature term from (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In Position-and-velocity joints (𝑖∈𝒥_𝑝) ‣ III-B Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Multiplying (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma_{p})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\alpha_{o}-\nu-\gamma_{p})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and rearranging gives the quadratic:</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E34X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{q}_{\max,i}\gamma_{p}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{q}_{\max,i}\gamma_{p}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34X.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\!\left(\dot{q}_{\max,i}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+\bar{C}_{1}\right)\gamma_{p}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\!\left(\dot{q}_{\max,i}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+\bar{C}_{1}\right)\gamma_{p}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S4.E34Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\left(\bar{C}_{1}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+\tfrac{2C_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)&lt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\left(\bar{C}_{1}(2\alpha_{o}-\nu)+\tfrac{2C_{2}}{\dot{q}_{\max,i}}\right)&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.10" class="ltx_p">By the same discriminant argument, a feasible interval <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}\in(\gamma_{p,\min},\gamma_{p,\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}\in(\gamma_{p,\min},\gamma_{p,\max})</annotation></semantics></math> exists when the joint limits outweigh the disturbance bounds. The additional curvature term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> narrows the feasible interval relative to the velocity case, requiring either a smaller <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> or larger <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> to compensate.
 ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>3 </span>MFCQ (Assumption <a href="#S4.I2.i4" title="item 4 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">MFCQ requires the existence of a strict descent direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all active constraints at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Acceleration bounds:</em> By <a href="#S4.I2.i3" title="item 3 ‣ Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the box constraints are strictly non-degenerate, so the upper and lower bounds cannot be simultaneously active for the same joint. An inward-pointing direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> trivially exists for any active face of the hyper-rectangle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Torque constraints:</em> These constraints are decoupled from the strict feasibility set via the independent slack variables <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any active torque constraint, a direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s,i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s,i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> strictly decreases the constraint residual without violating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Fix any inward-pointing direction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the active acceleration faces. For each torque constraint <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s,i}&gt;\left\|\hat{\mathbf{M}}_{i,:}\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s,i}&gt;\left\|\hat{\mathbf{M}}_{i,:}\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the directional derivative of the corresponding constraint is strictly negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">Combining <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{\ddot{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the positive slack directions yields a global <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that strictly decreases all active constraints, so MFCQ holds at <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{*}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tuning Guidelines</span>
</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> lists the main design parameters. The paragraphs below state the conditions behind the safety and stability guarantees in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>; choose <math id="S5.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the FLS structure so that <math id="S5.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on the intended operating envelope.</p>
</div>
