<td id="S5.T1.18.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_justify" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.18.18.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.18.18.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.18.18.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Conservative bound on </span><math id="S5.T1.18.18.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐝</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">˙</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.800em">CBF</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\dot{\mathbf{d}}_{\mathrm{CBF}}\|</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span>
</td>
</tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.19.19" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.19.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;"><math id="S5.T1.19.19.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.19.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;"><span id="S5.T1.19.19.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">QP</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.19.19.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_justify" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.19.19.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.19.19.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.19.19.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Exact-penalty weight on torque slack</span></span>
</span>
</td>
</tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.20.20" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.20.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;"><math id="S5.T1.20.20.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">ε</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.20.20.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;"><span id="S5.T1.20.20.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">QP</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.20.20.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_justify ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.65pt;padding-bottom:0.65pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.20.20.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T1.20.20.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.20.20.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Regularisation on the slack variables</span></span>
</span>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference model and adaptation.</span> The command-driven reference model requires positive-definite diagonal gains satisfying <math id="S5.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{v}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}+\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{v}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}+\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{p}=\mathbf{K}_{r}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{p}=\mathbf{K}_{r}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. The stability proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(iii) treats <math id="S5.p2.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{K}_{r},\boldsymbol{\Lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{K}_{r},\boldsymbol{\Lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> as the natural-frequency and damping of the filtered error dynamics; larger <math id="S5.p2.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> yields faster reference-model tracking but also amplifies the effect of any unmodelled QP correction through <math id="S5.p2.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{QP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>QP</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{QP}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The FLS and SMC gains <math id="S5.p2.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta},\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w},\sigma_{w},\varepsilon_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta},\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w},\sigma_{w},\varepsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> enter the same Lyapunov analysis: <math id="S5.p2.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚪</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> must be positive definite, <math id="S5.p2.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> should be kept small to limit parameter drift, and each <math id="S5.p2.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sets the residual approximation error that bounds the SMC layer in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ([21]) ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The compensation observer gain <math id="S5.p2.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> should be chosen large enough for effective disturbance rejection, but need only be consistent with the boundedness assumptions on <math id="S5.p2.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ III-C Fuzzy Logic and Disturbance Observer Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.20" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety and robust NCBF parameters.</span> State invariance (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(ii)) rests on Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 ([28]) ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and the hard CBF constraints (<a href="#S3.E22.2" title="In 22 ‣ III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22b</span></a>). The robust margin depends on <math id="S5.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu,\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu,\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> through (<a href="#S3.E22" title="In III-D Enforcing Constraints via Soft-Constrained Optimisation ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) and must satisfy <math id="S5.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\nu&lt;2\alpha_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\nu&lt;2\alpha_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math id="S5.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\}&lt;2\alpha_{o}-\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p}\}&lt;2\alpha_{o}-\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Increasing <math id="S5.p3.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or decreasing <math id="S5.p3.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> enlarges the margin <math id="S5.p3.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and makes the safety filter more conservative; increasing <math id="S5.p3.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> allows larger <math id="S5.p3.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> before the margin diverges. The NCBF rates <math id="S5.p3.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> should be selected inside the feasible intervals implied by (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>). A positive discriminant in each quadratic defines an admissible interval <math id="S5.p3.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma_{(\cdot),\min},\gamma_{(\cdot),\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma_{(\cdot),\min},\gamma_{(\cdot),\max})</annotation></semantics></math>; larger <math id="S5.p3.13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> improves constraint convergence but consumes more of the available acceleration box through <math id="S5.p3.14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>marg</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{marg},i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For position-constrained joints, the exponent <math id="S5.p3.15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In Position-and-velocity joints (𝑖∈𝒥_𝑝) ‣ III-B Safety-Filtered Reference Model and NCBF Design ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) trades approximation of the true safe set against curvature of the barrier: larger even <math id="S5.p3.16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S5.p3.17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}(l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}(l)</annotation></semantics></math> approach <math id="S5.p3.18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also increases the term <math id="S5.p3.19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In IV-A2 Uniform Feasibility (Assumption 3) ‣ IV-A Verification of Lipschitz Continuity ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) and narrows the feasible range of <math id="S5.p3.20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QP feasibility and torque constraints.</span> Uniform feasibility of the hard CBF box (Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(P3)) requires, for every joint <math id="S5.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, that the robust lower and upper acceleration bounds remain strictly separated: <math id="S5.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{lower},i}(\mathbf{x})&lt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{upper},i}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>lower</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>upper</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{lower},i}(\mathbf{x})&lt;\ddot{q}_{\mathrm{upper},i}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in the operating set. This condition is most restrictive near the constraint boundary and for joints in <math id="S5.p4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, where both position and velocity limits interact. When a feasible interval for <math id="S5.p4.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, any interior choice preserves a nonempty hard constraint set for the QP. Torque satisfaction whenever hard feasibility holds (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(i)) requires the exact penalty weight <math id="S5.p4.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to exceed the optimal Lagrange multiplier of the hard-constrained problem; in practice, <math id="S5.p4.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> should be increased until torque slack remains inactive during nominal operation. The regularisation <math id="S5.p4.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{s}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ensures strong convexity of the QP and the local Lipschitz continuity of <math id="S5.p4.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>cmd</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{cmd}}</annotation></semantics></math>; it should be kept small so that slack activation remains a last resort rather than a persistent softening of the torque bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operating regime.</span> Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> applies on <math id="S5.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the interaction wrench exceeds what the torque limits can support, slack activation is unavoidable; begin tuning from the joint limits and the feasible intervals for <math id="S5.p5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu,\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p},l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu,\beta,\bar{\omega}_{1},\alpha_{o},\gamma_{v},\gamma_{p},l)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S5.F2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:433.6pt;">
<foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:99.3em;--ltx-fo-height:52.4em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;" height="524.0pt" width="993.0pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 725)" overflow="visible" class="ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2605.28367v2/Images/simulation_results_1.png" id="S5.F2.1.1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="1374" height="725" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S5.F2.22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>: Impedance tracking error <math id="S5.F2.9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> for the proposed controller (green), AWORM (red), and NIC (blue). The shaded region denotes the interval of interaction wrenches. <span id="S5.F2.23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>: Joint 2 control torques under nominal limits. Dashed lines show <math id="S5.F2.10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>; the green region indicates torque constraint violations. <span id="S5.F2.24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>: Minimum NCBF over all joints. Dashed horizontal lines show the safety threshold; the green region indicates safety violations. <span id="S5.F2.25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>: Norm of the reference-model learning error <math id="S5.F2.11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> for the proposed controller. <span id="S5.F2.26.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>: Applied wrench norm <math id="S5.F2.12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> against the wrench metric <math id="S5.F2.13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In III-E Feasibility Metric ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). <span id="S5.F2.27.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>: RMS impedance tracking error over the full simulation horizon as model mismatch increases from <math id="S5.F2.14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.F2.15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Simulation Validation</span>
</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setup</span>
</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the proposed controller on a simulated KINOVA Gen3 7-DOF manipulator. The true plant follows (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with manufacturer inertial parameters. In simulation, the disturbance is set to joint friction, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)=\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{fric}}(\dot{\mathbf{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>fric</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{d}(t)=\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{fric}}(\dot{\mathbf{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, modelled by the smooth element-wise approximation</p>
<table id="S6.E35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.E35X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E35X.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau_{\mathrm{fric},i}(\dot{q}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>fric</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau_{\mathrm{fric},i}(\dot{q}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E35X.3.2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl(f_{c,i}+(f_{s,i}-f_{c,i})e^{-(\dot{q}_{i}/v_{s,i})^{2}}\bigr)\tanh(\kappa\dot{q}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl(f_{c,i}+(f_{s,i}-f_{c,i})e^{-(\dot{q}_{i}/v_{s,i})^{2}}\bigr)\tanh(\kappa\dot{q}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td>
</tr>
<tr id="S6.E35Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E35Xa.2.1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+f_{v,i}\dot{q}_{i},\qquad i\in[n]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+f_{v,i}\dot{q}_{i},\qquad i\in[n],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S6.SS1.p1.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=25</annotation></semantics></math> rad<sup id="S6.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-1</span></sup>s. The coefficients are arbitrary but physically reasonable joint-wise values, not identified parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The controller uses only (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Problem Formulation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbf{M}},\hat{\mathbf{C}},\hat{\mathbf{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbf{M}},\hat{\mathbf{C}},\hat{\mathbf{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by uniformly perturbing each physical parameter by up to <math id="S6.SS1.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 50\%</annotation></semantics></math>; neither (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In VI-A Setup ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) nor <math id="S6.SS1.p2.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{fric}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mi>fric</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\mathrm{fric}}</annotation></semantics></math> appears in the nominal model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.13" class="ltx_p">The task is to regulate a fixed end-effector pose <math id="S6.SS1.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> through the desired impedance model <math id="S6.SS1.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{d}=\mathbf{I}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{d}=\mathbf{I}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p3.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{d}=\operatorname{diag}(49\mathbf{I}_{3},16\mathbf{I}_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{d}=\operatorname{diag}(49\mathbf{I}_{3},16\mathbf{I}_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{d}=\operatorname{diag}(49\mathbf{I}_{3},12\mathbf{I}_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{d}=\operatorname{diag}(49\mathbf{I}_{3},12\mathbf{I}_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> while rejecting a measured human wrench. The interaction force <math id="S6.SS1.p3.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> has peak components <math id="S6.SS1.p3.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[20,20,-15]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[20,20,-15]</annotation></semantics></math> N along the end-effector translational axes, zero moment components, and is shaped by a half-sine pulse with a Gaussian envelope over <math id="S6.SS1.p3.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[5,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[5,7]</annotation></semantics></math> s. All simulations use a fixed-step RK4 integrator with <math id="S6.SS1.p3.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=10</annotation></semantics></math> ms over <math id="S6.SS1.p3.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> s, starting from <math id="S6.SS1.p3.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}(0)=[0;-1.0;1.0;-0.5;0.5;-1.2;0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}(0)=[0;-1.0;1.0;-0.5;0.5;-1.2;0]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{q}}(0)=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{q}}(0)=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S6.SS1.p3.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Impedance tracking performance is reported as <math id="S6.SS1.p3.13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>, with orientation evaluated via the quaternion error vector used in simulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constraints.</span> Joints <math id="S6.SS1.p4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> are subject to position and velocity limits (<math id="S6.SS1.p4.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{p}=\{2,4,6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{p}=\{2,4,6\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\max,i}=[126^{\circ},147^{\circ},117^{\circ}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>126</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>147</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>117</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\max,i}=[126^{\circ},147^{\circ},117^{\circ}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}_{\max,i}=[17,17,25]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}_{\max,i}=[17,17,25]</annotation></semantics></math> rpm); all other joints have velocity limits only (<math id="S6.SS1.p4.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{v}=\{1,3,5,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{v}=\{1,3,5,7\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}_{\max,i}=[17,17,25,25]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}_{\max,i}=[17,17,25,25]</annotation></semantics></math> rpm). Unless stated otherwise, torque limits are <math id="S6.SS1.p4.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 32</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.SS1.p4.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS1.p4.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 13</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.SS1.p4.13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS1.p4.14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p5.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Controller parameters.</span> The IT2-FLS uses four inputs per joint, <math id="S6.SS1.p5.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sin q_{i},\cos q_{i},\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sin q_{i},\cos q_{i},\dot{q}_{i},\bar{\xi}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, three Gaussian membership functions per input (<math id="S6.SS1.p5.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=81</annotation></semantics></math> rules), and first-order Sugeno consequents with five parameters per rule, giving <math id="S6.SS1.p5.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2835" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2835</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2835</annotation></semantics></math> adapted parameters in total. The reference-model and adaptation gains are <math id="S6.SS1.p5.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{r}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}=\sqrt{250}\,\mathbf{I}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>250</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{r}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}=\sqrt{250}\,\mathbf{I}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. The NCBF uses <math id="S6.SS1.p5.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p5.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{p}=\gamma_{v}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{p}=\gamma_{v}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p5.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=35</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p5.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}_{1}=75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}_{1}=75</annotation></semantics></math>. The DOB gains are <math id="S6.SS1.p5.9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}=70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}=70</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p5.10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{o}=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{o}=80</annotation></semantics></math>; the SMC leakage is <math id="S6.SS1.p5.11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{w}=0.0125" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{w}=0.0125</annotation></semantics></math>. The soft-constrained QP uses <math id="S6.SS1.p5.12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p5.13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{s}=10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{s}=10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is solved online in MATLAB with qpOASES and warm-starting at default tolerances; the average qpOASES solve time is <math id="S6.SS1.p5.14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.4\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.4\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> s per control step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare against:</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NIC</span>: a nominal impedance controller using only the perturbed model, with no FLS, DOB, or safety filter;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AWORM</span>: the same FLS, DOB, and safety filter as the proposed method, but with adaptation driven by the impedance filtered error <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\dot{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathrm{imp}}+\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\dot{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathrm{imp}}+\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{\mathrm{imp}}=\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{imp}}-\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than the filtered reference-model tracking error <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scenarios.</span> Four simulation studies are reported:</p>
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Nominal comparison</em> (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>A–C): all three controllers under the nominal torque limits above;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Model-mismatch sweep</em> (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>F): proposed controller only, with perturbation level varied from <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I2.i3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bursting comparison</em> (Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A–B): proposed versus AWORM with tightened limits of <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 22.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>22.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 22.5</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id="S6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I2.i4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Infeasibility test</em> (Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>C–F): proposed controller only, with limits further reduced to <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 15</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.I2.i4.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span>
</h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>1 </span>Nominal Comparison</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>A compares the impedance tracking error <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> for all three controllers under the nominal torque limits. The NIC performs significantly worse throughout the run. Without adaptation or a safety filter, it cannot compensate for the combined effect of the <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> parametric mismatch and the unmodeled friction (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In VI-A Setup ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), so its computed torques are systematically biased and impedance regulation degrades before and during the interaction interval. Both adaptive controllers achieve much lower error because the IT2-FLS and DOB learn and reject the resulting structured and unstructured residuals online; the proposed method tracks most closely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>B–C show that NIC violates torque and state constraints, whereas both adaptive controllers remain within bounds and maintain <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{i}{{}^{i}h(\mathbf{x}_{i})}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{i}{{}^{i}h(\mathbf{x}_{i})}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. NIC departs from <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> immediately because it has no safety filter and applies torques from the mismatched nominal model despite <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(0)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>2 </span>Effect of Model Mismatch</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V Tuning Guidelines ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>F reports the RMS impedance tracking error over the full <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> s trajectory as parameter perturbation increases from <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Unmodeled friction (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In VI-A Setup ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) is present in every run, so the error grows with mismatch but remains on the order of <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> even at <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>3 </span>Constraint-Aware Adaptation versus AWORM Bursting</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A–B isolates the effect of the command-driven reference model by comparing the proposed controller against AWORM with torque limits of <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 22.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>22.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 22.5</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. Both controllers share the same FLS, DOB, and safety filter; the only difference is the signal used for adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">AWORM exhibits the bursting behaviour characteristic of constrained learning-based systems. When the NCBFs activate, the safety filter suppresses motion and the impedance error grows. Because AWORM updates the FLS on <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it interprets this constraint-induced deficit as impedance-model tracking error and adapts against the safety filter. When the state re-enters the interior of <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the stored adaptation energy is released abruptly, producing the high-frequency torque oscillations in Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>B and the degraded tracking in Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The proposed controller avoids this failure mode because adaptation is driven by the filtered reference-model tracking error <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p3.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{imp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, keeping the torque command smooth and the impedance error lower than AWORM throughout the interaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>4 </span>Performance Under Infeasibility</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.8" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>C–F evaluates the proposed controller under limits of <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 15</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, a regime where the hard torque QP with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is frequently infeasible during interaction even though the hard CBF acceleration box remains nonempty (Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(P3)). Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VI-B4 Performance Under Infeasibility ‣ VI-B Results ‣ VI Simulation Validation ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>C compares the applied wrench <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> against the online wrench metric <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E27" title="In III-E Feasibility Metric ‣ III Methodology ‣ Safety-Critical Adaptive Impedance Control via Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions under State and Input Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). Whenever <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|&gt;F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|&gt;F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, no acceleration can satisfy both the hard CBF bounds and the zero-slack torque constraints, and the soft-constrained QP must activate <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}^{*}&gt;\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}^{*}&gt;\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Under repeated torque infeasibility, impedance tracking and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> remain bounded, torques stay smooth, and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p2.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}(t)\in\mathcal{C}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> because the CBF constraints remain hard.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S6.F3.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:433.6pt;">
<foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:99.3em;--ltx-fo-height:52.4em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;" height="524.0pt" width="993.0pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 725)" overflow="visible" class="ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2605.28367v2/Images/simulation_results_2.png" id="S6.F3.1.1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="1374" height="725" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S6.F3.32.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>: Impedance tracking error <math id="S6.F3.13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> for the proposed controller (green) and AWORM (red) with torque limits of <math id="S6.F3.14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 22.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>22.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 22.5</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.F3.15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.F3.16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.F3.33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>: Joint 2 torques under the same limits; the green region indicates violations. AWORM exhibits high-frequency chattering and transient torque violations. <span id="S6.F3.34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>–<span id="S6.F3.35.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>: Proposed controller with further tightened limits of <math id="S6.F3.17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 15</annotation></semantics></math> Nm on joints <math id="S6.F3.18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S6.F3.19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.F3.36.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>: <math id="S6.F3.20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{F}_{h}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S6.F3.21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}(\mathbf{x}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating hard-constraint infeasibility when the applied wrench exceeds the metric. <span id="S6.F3.37.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>: Impedance tracking error <math id="S6.F3.22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>imp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{imp}}(t)-\mathbf{z}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.F3.38.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>: Reference-model learning error <math id="S6.F3.23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\mathbf{r}(t)\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.F3.39.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>: Joint torques; the proposed controller remains smooth with no chattering.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span>
</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We presented a safety-critical online adaptive impedance controller combining a composed position-velocity NCBF, IT2-FLS adaptation with a command-driven reference model, and a soft-constrained QP with exact penalties. On <math id="S7.p1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{F}_{h})\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the closed loop keeps <math id="S7.p1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, renders <math id="S7.p1.3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant, and yields UUB impedance-tracking error. Simulations on a 7-DOF KINOVA arm with <math id="S7.p1.4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> parametric mismatch, unmodeled friction, and a pulsed interaction wrench showed improved tracking over NIC and AWORM, robustness to increasing mismatch, elimination of AWORM bursting, and smooth behaviour under tightened torque limits monitored by <math id="S7.p1.5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future work will investigate neural-network function approximation, task-space safety constraints, and experimental validation on additional manipulators.</p>
</div>
</section>
